{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>When you come to the land of Canaan which I am giving you as a possession.</b> One must examine why Scripture specified concerning the plagues of houses 'when you come to the land of Canaan' more than for the other kinds of plagues. Also the phrase 'which I am giving you as a possession' is entirely superfluous, for this is well known. Moreover, when it says 'and I will place a plague of tzara'at in a house of the land of your possession,' it should have said 'in your houses.' Even though according to Rashi's explanation, who explained that the Emorites hid away treasures, etc., this is not a difficulty — for he used 'your possession' to exclude the houses that Israel would build there, which have no relevance to that reason — nevertheless the language is somewhat difficult. For in saying 'which I am giving you as a possession,' it attributes the possession to HaShem, blessed be He, whereas in saying 'your possession' it attributes the possession to Israel, as though they are the ones who seize hold of the inheritance by the power of their hand. Also, Rashi's reason itself requires explanation." "And what seems right to me to say on this matter is that the principal reason is on account of narrowness of eye, as our Sages, blessed be their memory, said (Arakhin 16a) from the verse 'and he who has the house shall come' — this refers to one who set apart his house for himself alone and did not let others benefit from it. For this reason HaShem gave him, as a possession, a house full of every good thing, to test him whether he would do good from his house also for others — for 'the silver is Mine and the gold, says HaShem' (Haggai 2:8), and whatever a person gives to others, he gives not from his own but is merely meriting it from the table on high. Therefore it is said: 'When you come to the land of Canaan which I am giving you as a possession' — for not by their sword did they possess the land, nor did their arm save them (Psalms 44:3), but the right hand of HaShem was exalted, to give them the inheritance of nations. And there is no room for the narrow of eye to say 'my power and the might of my hand made me all this wealth,' since HaShem is the One who gives you power and possession; and if so, it is only just that you give from what is His to the poor of His people. And if you do not obey the voice of His word and become among the narrow of eye who attribute the possession to themselves, then 'and I will place a plague of tzara'at in a house of the land of your possession' — that is to say, in the place where you attribute the possession to yourselves, as though you are the ones who seize hold of it by the power of your hand. Therefore it is said immediately afterward: 'and he who has the house shall come' — this refers to one who set apart his house for himself alone, to say that by his power and the might of his hand he built his house. Or one may say 'your possession' — this refers to one who set apart his house for himself and does not let others benefit from it; 'and he who has the house shall come' — this refers to one who set apart his house for himself literally as stated. For both speak to this matter: either from one who hangs the possession upon himself, from which narrowness of eye follows, or both speak of narrowness of eye drawn from hanging the possession upon oneself." "And there are those who say that the reason for the plagues of houses is that the Emorites built their houses for the sake of idolatry, and therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, brings plagues upon the houses in order to demolish them. And with this, it seems to me possible to settle the midrash that says: 'like a plague appears to me in the house' — this is idolatry (Yalkut Shimoni, Metzora 528), and it interpreted this entire passage as referring to the Holy Temple that was destroyed on account of the idolatry that they set up there, etc. For would it enter the mind to say that the author of this midrash intended to remove the entire passage from its plain sense and interpret it concerning the destruction of the Holy Temple? Rather, it is certain that his intent is also to give a reason for the plague of the house, because the Emorites built it for the sake of idolatry, and therefore it comes to be demolished. And from here he learns to say that also the Holy Temple, in which Menasseh set up the idolatrous image, came to be demolished on account of this sin; and all the details stated here were also found in the destruction of the House. Just as it is stated here 'and he who has the house shall come,' so too in the destruction of the Holy Temple, 'he who has the house shall come' — this is the Holy One, blessed be He. And just as it is stated here 'and he shall tell the priest,' so too the Holy One, blessed be He, will tell Jeremiah, who was of the priests that were in Anatot. And just as it is stated here 'like a plague appears to me,' so too the Holy One, blessed be He, will say. And in this manner the entire midrash and all the verses are explained according to their plain sense." "And one should not object to this reason: if so, why do the plagues not come upon the houses immediately when they enter the land? According to the reason of hidden treasures, it is fine, for perhaps not every homeowner merits that treasure. The answer to this is that also in the elimination of idolatry, merit is drawn through the merit of the righteous, and specifically if a worthy man, virtuous in his deeds, comes to dwell in the house, it is not fitting that he should dwell in a house that bears the taint of idolatry. And we may say that this verse is good tidings for Israel, as it is said (Lamentations 4:11) 'HaShem has spent His wrath and kindled a fire in Zion,' and this good is that He spends His wrath on wood and stones and does not touch the bodies. So too this good news concerning the plagues of houses is what is meant by 'like a plague appears to me in the house' — with the kaf of comparison: like the plague that was fit to come upon me, appears to me in the house. Also according to the reason of narrowness of eye, He said: like the plague fit to come upon the narrow of eye, namely the scattering of their wealth, so 'appears to me in the house' — for in the end it comes to pass that the priest scatters his wealth. And the fact that He commanded to take birds to purge the house — we have already said that this is to hint that the wealth one makes for himself grows wings like flying birds; but according to Rashi, who mentioned only the matter of grinding the clay, what relevance does this have to the plagues of houses? Therefore we said above that the birds contain an allusion also to the coveting of money that causes haughtiness of heart, and therefore he must also take cedar wood."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:53.255969263Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}